  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and state-broadcaster IRIB covered the Sep 1 ballistic missile launch as a "measured and proportionate defensive response" to CENTCOM strikes, framing it as a closed chapter rather than the opening of a new cycle. Supreme Leader Khamenei's office published a statement characterizing the Qeshm and Jask strikes as "criminal aggression" and confirming Iran's right to continued response — stopping short of a timetable or escalatory pledge. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>Tasnim and Fars gave prominent coverage to AEOI Chief's Aug 26 doctrine barring IAEA access to war-damaged sites, running it again Sep 1-2 alongside Khamenei's restatement that nuclear programme continuity is a sovereign matter not subject to "wartime pressure." The IAEA DG 'urgent need' language was dismissed by IRIB as "Western-coordinated political pressure." → supports <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Iranian state media carried no MFA formal statement on Hormuz preconditions during Sep 1-2. Kayhan's editorial framed Hormuz closure as Iran's "strategic depth" and a permanent card, not a concession to negotiate. Press TV noted the US-allied Jordan and UAE strikes without amplifying IRGC casualty or damage figures. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

